`make` builds four standalone sequence generators in this directory.

`./spi`

Generates the original Pmod DA3 transaction and writes the PulsePins text sequence to `sequence`.
For testing, plug the Pmod DA3 module into the first Pmod connector, upper row.

`./spi_ad9833`

Generates an AD9833 programming sequence for a 5 MHz sine output and writes the PulsePins text sequence to `sequence`.

AD9833 module wiring on `qout`:

* `qout[16]` -> module `CS` (MCP41010 chip-select, held high)
* `qout[17]` -> module `DAT`
* `qout[18]` -> module `CLK`
* `qout[19]` -> module `FSY` / `FSYNC`

The AD9833 binary uses SPI mode `2` and sends these 16-bit words to the AD9833:

* `0x2100`
* `0x7333`
* `0x4ccc`
* `0xc000`
* `0x2000`

`./spi_ad9833_pga`

Generates the same AD9833 5 MHz sine setup as `./spi_ad9833`, then programs the module's onboard MCP41010-controlled amplifier stage.

The DollaTek board does not use a dedicated programmable-gain amplifier IC. Instead, the MCP41010 digital potentiometer drives the AD8051 amplified-output stage as a variable attenuator into a fixed op-amp gain of about 6×.

Supported ranges for the amplified output are therefore approximate:

* MCP41010 wiper code: `0x00..0xff`
* module amplified-output gain relative to raw AD9833 `VOUT`: about 0× to 6×

The top of that range is not calibrated and can clip depending on frequency, supply, and load. Treat the gain numbers as wiring-level estimates for this module, not guaranteed analog amplitudes.

The default programmed by `./spi_ad9833_pga` is approximately 5× amplified output, which maps to MCP41010 wiper code `0xd5` and SPI word `0x11d5`.

The combined binary uses two device-specific transactions on the shared bus:

* AD9833 on `FSY` using SPI mode `2`
* MCP41010 on `CS` using SPI mode `3`

Manual testing:

* copy the generated `sequence` file to the board
* run `pptest 42 -f sequence -veryverbose`

`./spi_ad9959 [output.seq [CH0_Hz CH1_Hz CH2_Hz CH3_Hz]]`

Generates a one-shot AD9959 setup sequence. The default file is `ad9959.seq`;
CH0, CH1, CH2 and CH3 default to 50, 75, 100 and 125 MHz. Frequencies are numeric
Hz (scientific notation such as `50e6` is accepted), positive and at most 200 MHz;
values that round to DC are rejected. All four frequencies must be supplied
together. `--help` prints usage.

Clock assumptions: PulsePins streamer at 100 MHz, DDS onboard reference at
25 MHz, PLL multiplier x20 (500 MHz DDS system clock). The generator reports
the tuning words and nominal realized frequencies; oscillator error is additional.

Wiring follows Section 9 of `extra/AD9959_DDS_module_bench_manual.pdf`:

| PulsePins bit | DDS signal | DDS DATA pin | PP_PMOD KiCad pad |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Q0 | SCLK | 12 | J3.11 |
| Q1 | CS | 10 | J3.9 |
| Q2 | SDIO_0 | 9 | J3.7 |
| Q3 | SDIO_1 | 11 | J3.5 |
| Q4 | SDIO_2 | 13 | J3.12 |
| Q5 | SDIO_3 | 14 | J3.10 |
| Q6 | I/O_UPDATE | 8 | J3.8 |
| Q7 | MASTER_RESET | 6 | J3.6 |
| Q8..Q11 | P0..P3 | 1, 3, 5, 7 | J4.11, J4.9, J4.7, J4.5 |
| Q12 | PWR_DWN_CTL | 4 | J4.12 |
| GND | GND | 2 | J3/J4.3 or .4 |

These are KiCad pad numbers, not standard Pmod numbers. KiCad pads 1 and 2
on J3/J4 are +3.3 V and remain unconnected to the separately powered DDS.
Power the DDS before enabling the PulsePins outputs.

The sequence uses single-bit, 2-wire SPI mode 0 at 12.5 MHz. SDIO_1..3,
profile pins, and power-down remain low. Q13..Q31 are also driven low.
It pulses reset for 1 us, writes FR1=0xD00000, pulses UPDATE for 1 us,
and waits 2 ms for the PLL. It then sets single-tone sine mode, zero phase
offsets, full DAC current and amplitude code 1023, programs each channel's
32-bit frequency word, and applies all channels with one shared UPDATE.
The final bus value is 0x2 (CS high, all other bits low). The DDS continues
generating after playback exits. RF amplitude depends on the module, frequency,
and load; amplitude code 1023 is not a calibrated voltage setting.

Build and test on the development host:

```sh
make -C tools/spi_payload spi_ad9959 check-ad9959
tools/spi_payload/spi_ad9959 /tmp/ad9959.seq
scp /tmp/ad9959.seq root@BOARD_IP:ad9959.seq
```

On the board, confirm the reported streamer clock is 100 MHz, then play once:

```sh
./ppreset -i 0x2 -int_clk
./ppplay -file ad9959.seq -format text -i 0x2 -force -check -hard-timeout 5s
```

The in-file `final 0x2` selects the persistent final state; omit `-t`,
`-random_final`, and `PP_RANDOM_FINAL`. For a 100 MHz streamer the default
sequence takes about 2 ms, including PLL settling. `-check` verifies the digital
programming waveform through PulsePins readback, not AD9959 register readback
or RF output. Measure CH0..CH3 on a scope/analyzer to confirm the actual tones;
the DDS SYNC_CLK should be 125 MHz after the PLL setup.

To blank all four DDS outputs, drive PWR_DWN_CTL high with CS still high:

```sh
./ppreset -i 0x1002
```

Replay the generated sequence to reinitialize and resume the tones.

### Test 1: CH1 frequency staircase

`./spi_ad9959 --test1 [output.seq]` writes `ad9959_test1.seq` by default.
After the standard four-channel initialization, CH1 cycles through 50, 75,
100 and 125 MHz six times, dwelling 1 s at each frequency (plus a few
microseconds for programming). After about 24 s, CH1 returns to 75 MHz.
CH0, CH2 and CH3 stay at 50, 100 and 125 MHz during the staircase.

On a Siglent SVA1015X connected to CH1, use Spectrum Analyzer mode, center
87.5 MHz, span 100 MHz, RBW 100 kHz, VBW 300 kHz, reference level 0 dBm,
10 dB/div, input attenuation 20 dB, preamp off, positive-peak detector,
continuous sweep, free trigger and automatic sweep time. Trace 1 in Clear Write shows the
hopping carrier; optional Trace 2 in Max Hold collects all four peaks.
Configure the analyzer before starting playback.

```sh
./spi_ad9959 --test1 /tmp/ad9959_test1.seq
scp /tmp/ad9959_test1.seq root@BOARD_IP:ad9959_test1.seq
```

On the board:

```sh
./ppplay -file ad9959_test1.seq -format text -i 0x2 -force -check -timeout 3 -hard-timeout 35s
```

Keep `-check` enabled for this long sequence: the readback check waits through
the programmed dwells before the normal 10 s completion wait starts. The
3 s idle timeout allows the 1 s gaps between SPI transactions. A successful
run checks the complete digital waveform and final qout=0x2; confirm the four
RF frequencies and the final 75 MHz carrier on the analyzer separately.

### Test 2: CH1 two-level profile FSK

`./spi_ad9959 --test2 [output.seq]` writes `ad9959_test2.seq` by default.
It preloads CH1's CFTW0 with 70 MHz and CW1 with 80 MHz, enables two-level
frequency modulation, and toggles P1 (Q9 on J4) every 500 ms for 24 cycles.
There is a 10 ms idle pause between standard initialization and FSK setup to
let the board's readback FIFO drain before the next burst of SPI transitions.
The 24 s modulation interval contains only profile-pin changes. It then
restores single-tone operation at 75 MHz. CH0, CH2 and CH3 stay at their
standard 50, 100 and 125 MHz settings during modulation.

Use the same SVA1015X settings as Test 1, except center 75 MHz and span
20 MHz (65-85 MHz). Clear Write shows the alternating 70/80 MHz peaks.
Max Hold retains both; it can also retain the 75 MHz carrier from before
or after the test. Configure the analyzer before playback.

```sh
./spi_ad9959 --test2 /tmp/ad9959_test2.seq
scp /tmp/ad9959_test2.seq root@BOARD_IP:ad9959_test2.seq
```

On the board, use the same long-sequence readback timeout policy as Test 1:

```sh
./ppplay -file ad9959_test2.seq -format text -i 0x2 -force -check -timeout 3 -hard-timeout 35s
```

### Test 3: CH1 sixteen-level profile FSK

`./spi_ad9959 --test3 [output.seq]` writes `ad9959_test3.seq` by default.
CH1's 16 frequency words cover 60 through 90 MHz in 2 MHz increments. The
profile pins select this scrambled order, at 250 ms per frequency:

```text
60 74 64 86 70 80 62 90 76 66 84 72 88 68 82 78 MHz
```

The four-second pattern repeats six times, then CH1 returns to 75 MHz in
single-tone mode. CH0, CH2 and CH3 retain their standard frequencies during
the hopping interval. FR1=0xD01300 assigns all four profile pins to CH1 with
16-level modulation; [P0:P3] is MSB-first, mapping Q8 to the profile MSB and
Q11 to the LSB. Initialization includes 10 ms drain pauses between groups of
profile writes to bound the readback FIFO occupancy. There are no SPI writes
during the 24 s hopping interval.

On the SVA1015X, use center 75 MHz and span 40 MHz (55-95 MHz). Keep the
bandwidth, amplitude, detector and sweep settings from Test 1. Trace 1/A
in Clear Write shows the hops; Trace 2/B in Max Hold collects the 16 peaks
at 60, 62, ..., 90 MHz. Max Hold may also retain the initial/final 75 MHz
carrier. Configure the analyzer before starting playback.

```sh
./spi_ad9959 --test3 /tmp/ad9959_test3.seq
scp /tmp/ad9959_test3.seq root@BOARD_IP:ad9959_test3.seq
```

On the board:

```sh
./ppplay -file ad9959_test3.seq -format text -i 0x2 -force -check -timeout 3 -hard-timeout 35s
```

### Test 4: CH1 asymmetric triangular frequency sweep

`./spi_ad9959 --test4 [output.seq]` writes `ad9959_test4.seq` by default.
It uses the AD9959 linear frequency sweep engine, with endpoints 60/90 MHz,
RDW=516, FDW=172, and LSRR=0xFAFA. Both step intervals are 2 us; the frequency
steps are approximately 60.0703 Hz up and 20.0234 Hz down. The endpoint times
are approximately 0.998830 s and 2.996490 s. P1 stays high for 1 s, then low
for 3 s, allowing the DDS to reach and briefly hold each endpoint. Six cycles
run for 24 s before CH1 returns to single-tone 75 MHz. The other channels
retain their standard frequencies during the sweep interval.

For a clear view of the unequal sweep slopes on the SVA1015X, configure:

* Center 75 MHz, span 0 Hz (Zero Span).
* RBW 1 MHz, VBW 1 MHz, sweep time 4 s, continuous sweep, free trigger.
* Reference 0 dBm, 10 dB/div, attenuation 20 dB, preamp off.
* Trace 1/A Clear Write with Pos Peak; turn other traces off/Blank.

The display is now power versus time. Each four-second cycle crosses 75 MHz
twice: a narrow rounded pulse on the upward sweep and a pulse about three
times wider on the downward sweep. Their approximate 3 dB widths are 33 ms
and 100 ms for a 1 MHz RBW. The peaks are about 2 s apart. Before and after
the test, the steady 75 MHz carrier produces a flat high trace.

```sh
./spi_ad9959 --test4 /tmp/ad9959_test4.seq
scp /tmp/ad9959_test4.seq root@BOARD_IP:ad9959_test4.seq
```

On the board, allow more than 3 s between readback transitions:

```sh
./ppplay -file ad9959_test4.seq -format text -i 0x2 -force -check -timeout 5 -hard-timeout 35s
```

### Test 5: CH1 retriggered no-dwell frequency sweep

`./spi_ad9959 --test5 [output.seq]` writes `ad9959_test5.seq` by default.
CH1 sweeps from 60 to 90 MHz in approximately 0.499416 s, then immediately
jumps back to 60 MHz and waits for the next trigger. The no-dwell bit is
enabled (CFR=0x80E301), with RDW=1032 and a 2 us rising step interval.
P1 stays high for 750 ms, then low for 250 ms, providing a fresh rising edge
every second. The return happens while P1 is still high; no-dwell does not
automatically repeat the sweep. Falling sweep parameters are unused.
After 24 cycles, CH1 returns to single-tone 75 MHz. CH0, CH2 and CH3 retain
their standard frequencies during the sweep interval. Initialization uses
a 10 ms readback drain pause as in Test 4.

Keep the Test 4 SVA1015X settings (75 MHz center, Zero Span, RBW/VBW 1 MHz,
Clear Write/Pos Peak, other traces Blank), but change sweep time to 2 s.
Expect equally spaced crossing pulses once per second, approximately 17 ms
wide at 3 dB below their peaks. Each two-second display shows about two
pulses. The steady 75 MHz carrier before and after produces a flat high trace.

```sh
./spi_ad9959 --test5 /tmp/ad9959_test5.seq
scp /tmp/ad9959_test5.seq root@BOARD_IP:ad9959_test5.seq
```

On the board:

```sh
./ppplay -file ad9959_test5.seq -format text -i 0x2 -force -check -timeout 3 -hard-timeout 35s
```

### Test 6: CH1 digital amplitude staircase

`./spi_ad9959 --test6 [output.seq]` writes `ad9959_test6.seq` by default.
CH1 stays at 75 MHz while its manual amplitude scale factor steps through
1023, 512, 256, 128 and 64, holding each for 1 s plus programming time.
Relative to the initial full output, these are approximately 0, -6.012,
-12.033, -18.053 and -24.074 dB. The five-second pattern repeats five times,
then full amplitude is restored. The DAC current remains at its full setting;
CH0, CH2 and CH3 retain their standard tones during the staircase.
Initialization includes a 10 ms readback drain pause.

On the SVA1015X, configure:

* Center 75 MHz, span 0 Hz (Zero Span).
* RBW 100 kHz, VBW 300 kHz, sweep time 5 s, continuous sweep, free trigger.
* Reference 0 dBm, 10 dB/div, attenuation 20 dB, preamp off.
* Trace 1/A Clear Write with Pos Peak; other traces off/Blank.

Expect a repeating five-level staircase, falling by roughly 6 dB each second
before jumping back to the top. The level offsets are relative to full output,
not absolute dBm readings. Free triggering can place the start of the pattern
anywhere on the display. After about 25 s, the trace returns to its original
flat level.

```sh
./spi_ad9959 --test6 /tmp/ad9959_test6.seq
scp /tmp/ad9959_test6.seq root@BOARD_IP:ad9959_test6.seq
```

On the board:

```sh
./ppplay -file ad9959_test6.seq -format text -i 0x2 -force -check -timeout 3 -hard-timeout 35s
```

### Test 7: CH1 sampled sinusoidal AM

`./spi_ad9959 --test7 [output.seq]` writes `ad9959_test7.seq` by default.
CH1 stays at 75 MHz with a 1 kHz, 50%-depth amplitude envelope. Eight samples
of `round(682 + 341*cos(2*pi*k/8))` are preloaded into amplitude profiles.
FR1=0xD01200 assigns P0:P2 to CH1 in eight-level mode, with P0 the profile
MSB. Profile zero uses ACR[9:0]; CW1..CW7 hold MSB-aligned 10-bit amplitudes.
The other channels retain their standard tones during modulation.

The generated file uses eight PulsePins `store` slots and `r 24000 8` to
replay 125 us samples for 24 s, fitting the board's finite input buffers.
Every SPI transaction includes a 10 ms readback drain pause so verbose
capture can format and write each edge. The `tone_sequence` helper accepts
an optional `post_write_idle_ticks` argument for this; its default is 4 ticks.
After playback, CH1 returns to a full-amplitude, unmodulated 75 MHz carrier.

On the SVA1015X, return to a frequency-span display:

* Initially use center 75 MHz, span 100 kHz; Peak Search and Marker -> Center
  Frequency on the current carrier, then reduce span to 10 kHz. This follows
  the module's actual reference frequency rather than assuming exact 75 MHz.
* RBW 100 Hz, VBW 300 Hz, continuous sweep, automatic sweep time, free trigger.
* Reference 0 dBm, 10 dB/div, attenuation 20 dB, preamp off.
* Trace 1/A Clear Write with Pos Peak; other traces off/Blank.

Expect a carrier and two sidebands at +/-1 kHz, each approximately -12.3 dBc.
Ideal sinusoidal AM gives -12.04 dBc; the eight-sample zero-order hold adds
about 0.22 dB attenuation. The carrier itself drops about 3.52 dB relative to
the preceding full-amplitude tone because the mean amplitude is 682/1023.
Sampling images occur farther out (the first at +/-7 kHz), outside this span.

```sh
./spi_ad9959 --test7 /tmp/ad9959_test7.seq
scp /tmp/ad9959_test7.seq root@BOARD_IP:ad9959_test7.seq
```

The installed ABI 5 `ppplay -check` does not expand store/replay commands.
Capture with `-read` instead; this also drains readback during the 24 s run:

```sh
./ppplay -file ad9959_test7.seq -format text -i 0x2 -force -read -timeout 3 -hard-timeout 35s > ad9959_test7.log 2>&1
```

`-read` ends after 3 s without further captured data, printing an idle-timeout
message after the waveform finishes. Check the playback exit status and CRC,
then copy the log back and compare every captured run, including SPI setup
and restoration, against the locally expanded sequence:

```sh
scp root@BOARD_IP:ad9959_test7.log /tmp/ad9959_test7.log
python3 check_readback.py /tmp/ad9959_test7.seq /tmp/ad9959_test7.log
```

`check_readback.py` supports the generator's `d`, finite `store`/`r`, `final`
and `f` subset. It compares merged run lengths and pin values and verifies
the logged final output value. This complements the board's CRC/FIFO checks.

### Test 8: CH1 native binary phase switching

`./spi_ad9959 --test8 [output.seq]` writes `ad9959_test8.seq` by default.
CH1 stays at 75 MHz with full amplitude. Two-level phase modulation selects
0 degrees from CPOW0 or 180 degrees from CW1=0x80000000 (MSB-aligned 14-bit
phase). P1 selects the phase, holding each for 50 us: a 10 kHz repeating
pattern. Two stored pin states replay 30,000 times for a 3 s PSK block, then
P1 stays low for a 3 s plain-carrier block. Four pairs run for 24 s, followed
by restoration of single-tone mode. The other channels retain their standard
tones. Every SPI transaction has the same 10 ms capture-drain pause as Test 7.

The `sinusoidal_am_sequence()` and `phase_keying_sequence()` helpers return a
`ProfileSequence`: setup, stored period, repetitions per block, block count,
idle ticks after each block, and restoration. Idle blocks use the normal idle
pin value, selecting profile zero.

On the SVA1015X, keep the center on the actual carrier found in Test 7:

* Span 80 kHz, RBW 1 kHz, VBW 3 kHz.
* Continuous sweep, automatic sweep time, free trigger.
* Reference 0 dBm, 10 dB/div, attenuation 20 dB, preamp off.
* Trace 1/A Clear Write with Pos Peak; other traces off/Blank.

During PSK, the center carrier is ideally suppressed. The strongest lines
are at +/-10 kHz, each about 3.92 dB below the plain-carrier level. The next
pair at +/-30 kHz is about 9.54 dB below the first pair. During each plain
block, the center carrier returns. Clear Write lets you see the change every
3 s; after the test, the plain carrier remains.

```sh
./spi_ad9959 --test8 /tmp/ad9959_test8.seq
scp /tmp/ad9959_test8.seq root@BOARD_IP:ad9959_test8.seq
```

On the board, use quiet capture and allow more than 3 s between readback
transitions. Formatting all 20,000 transitions per second overflows readback
on the bench board, even with the log in RAM; `-quiet` drains without printing
individual runs:

```sh
./ppplay -file ad9959_test8.seq -format text -i 0x2 -force -read -quiet -timeout 5 -hard-timeout 35s > ad9959_test8.log 2>&1
```

As in Test 7, `-read` ends with an idle-timeout message after playback. Verify
the result locally using summary mode:

```sh
scp root@BOARD_IP:ad9959_test8.log /tmp/ad9959_test8.log
python3 check_readback.py --summary /tmp/ad9959_test8.seq /tmp/ad9959_test8.log
```

`--summary` checks the captured run count and duration, successful playback,
and final pins. It also computes the expected IEEE CRC-32 over every intended
32-bit little-endian pin sample and compares it with the hardware CRC. This
verifies the waveform against the input sequence without verbose capture.

### Test 9: CH1 abrupt versus automatically ramped bursts

`./spi_ad9959 --test9 [output.seq]` writes `ad9959_test9.seq` by default.
CH1 stays at 75 MHz. P1 alternates high for 10 ms and low for 10 ms, giving
50 bursts per second. Each case lasts 250 bursts (5 s): abrupt, ramped,
abrupt, ramped. Approximately 20 ms of SPI programming separates cases.
After about 20 s, full-amplitude single-tone output is restored. CH0, CH2
and CH3 retain their standard tones during the burst comparison.

Abrupt bursts use two-level amplitude modulation, with zero amplitude in
ACR and full amplitude in CW1. Ramped bursts use the single-tone automatic
RU/RD engine: ACR=0x3D1FFF enables the multiplier, automatic ramp and timer
reload, with ARR=61 and a 1-LSB increment/decrement. At SYNC_CLK=125 MHz,
1023 steps take approximately 0.499 ms. P1 high ramps up; P1 low ramps down.
The envelope ramps linearly in amplitude, so a logarithmic power display
shows curved edges. The `ProfileSequence.block_setup` vectors provide the
SPI mode changes before each replay block; an empty vector omits them.

Configure the SVA1015X:

* Keep the center on the actual 75 MHz carrier; span 0 Hz (Zero Span).
* RBW 1 MHz, VBW 1 MHz, sweep time 20 ms.
* Continuous sweep, free trigger; Trace 1/A Clear Write with Pos Peak.
* Reference 0 dBm, 10 dB/div, attenuation 20 dB, preamp off; other traces Blank.

Each screen covers about one on/off cycle. Look for sharp edges during the
first five seconds, sloped/curved edges for the next five, then a repeat of
both cases. Free triggering can place the edges anywhere on the display.
At the end, the trace returns to a flat full-amplitude level.

```sh
./spi_ad9959 --test9 /tmp/ad9959_test9.seq
scp /tmp/ad9959_test9.seq root@BOARD_IP:ad9959_test9.seq
```

On the board:

```sh
./ppplay -file ad9959_test9.seq -format text -i 0x2 -force -read -quiet -timeout 3 -hard-timeout 35s > ad9959_test9.log 2>&1
```

Use the same summary and independently calculated CRC check as Test 8:

```sh
scp root@BOARD_IP:ad9959_test9.log /tmp/ad9959_test9.log
python3 check_readback.py --summary /tmp/ad9959_test9.seq /tmp/ad9959_test9.log
```

### Test 10: CH1 DAC current versus digital scaling

`./spi_ad9959 --test10 [output.seq]` writes `ad9959_test10.seq` by default.
CH1 stays at 75 MHz while two four-second patterns alternate:

* DAC-current block: full, half, quarter, eighth current, 1 s per setting,
  with digital scale fixed at 1023. CFR[9:8] codes are 11, 01, 10, 00.
* Digital-scaling block: full DAC current, with amplitude scales 1023, 512,
  256 and 128, again 1 s per setting.

Three eight-second pairs run for about 24 s, then full current and digital
amplitude are restored. Both methods give approximately 0, -6, -12 and
-18 dB relative to full output. The DAC-current steps ideally differ by
6.0206 dB; digital rounding changes these comparisons by less than 0.01 dB.
Harmonic/spur behavior can differ because the two methods change different
parts of the signal path. CH0, CH2 and CH3 retain their standard tones during
the comparison. Each dwell includes a few microseconds of programming time.

On the SVA1015X, return to a frequency-span display:

* Center 125 MHz, span 250 MHz (0-250 MHz).
* RBW 100 kHz, VBW 300 kHz, continuous sweep, automatic sweep time, free trigger.
* Reference 0 dBm, 10 dB/div, attenuation 20 dB, preamp off.
* Trace 1/A Clear Write with Pos Peak; other traces off/Blank.

Watch the 75 MHz carrier descend through four levels. The first four seconds
use DAC-current scaling; the next four use digital scaling, and this pair
repeats three times. Compare any visible harmonics at 150 and 225 MHz and
other spurs at corresponding carrier levels. The RF measurement determines
whether their relative levels change; identical-looking traces are a valid
observation at the chosen bandwidth and display scale.

```sh
./spi_ad9959 --test10 /tmp/ad9959_test10.seq
scp /tmp/ad9959_test10.seq root@BOARD_IP:ad9959_test10.seq
```

This sequence uses regular data records and supports the board's full check:

```sh
./ppplay -file ad9959_test10.seq -format text -i 0x2 -force -check -timeout 3 -hard-timeout 35s
```

### Measured frequency/amplitude sweep: sweep1.py

`sweep1.py` controls PulsePins over SSH and the SVA1015X over LAN (SCPI TCP
5025). It sweeps CH1 through **100 equally spaced points including 0 and
200 MHz**, with a nominal total duration of **60 seconds**. The digital
amplitude decreases linearly with frequency from full scale to 10%:
`round(1023 * (1 - 0.9 * frequency / 200e6))`. The last code is 102, or
9.9707% of full scale, because of the DDS's 10-bit amplitude quantization.
This is digital voltage-amplitude scaling; the module's frequency response
also affects measured RF amplitude.

Run on the development computer, with the analyzer connected to CH1:

```sh
make -C tools/spi_payload spi_ad9959 check-ad9959 check-sweep1
python3 tools/spi_payload/sweep1.py
```

Defaults are PulsePins `root@192.168.3.47`, analyzer `192.168.3.55`, and output
`sweep1.dat` in the current directory. Requires Python 3, OpenSSH `ssh`/`scp`,
passwordless board access, and the board's existing `/home/root/ppplay`.
No Python packages, FPGA update or board-software installation are needed.
Options are `--board`, `--analyzer`, `--points`, `--duration`, and `--output`:

```sh
python3 tools/spi_payload/sweep1.py --points 100 --duration 60 --output sweep1.dat
```

The script initializes the standard four-channel tones, uploads short
per-point sequences once, and reuses an SSH connection. Each point is played
with `ppplay -check`, changing CH1 frequency and amplitude with one UPDATE.
Separate short playbacks fit the ABI 5 finite input buffer; the DDS stays
at the programmed setting while the analyzer measures it. CH0/CH2/CH3 stay
at their standard 50/100/125 MHz tones throughout the sweep.

The analyzer follows each setpoint in a 200 kHz span with 1 kHz RBW, 3 kHz
VBW, positive-peak detection, Clear Write, 20 dB attenuation, preamp off,
0 dBm reference and 10 dB/div. It acquires a fresh single scan, waits for
completion, then searches for and reads the peak marker. These are measured
trace peak positions, with resolution limited by the trace grid and RBW.
The script allows additional trace-publication time after scan completion
and retries invalid markers with up to three fresh scans at the same DDS
setting (the bench firmware sometimes returns a transient zero marker).
At least 600 ms is allocated per point by default, including programming
and measurement. If a point takes longer, the script extends the sweep
rather than advancing the DDS during acquisition. Initialization/upload
time is additional; the actual sweep duration is recorded in the file.

The ASCII file contains exactly two columns, both in Hz:

```text
# set_frequency_Hz measured_frequency_Hz
0.000000000 nan
```

There is one row per point, including `nan` at DC (0 Hz has no RF carrier).
Peaks below -80 dBm, non-finite readings, or peaks more than 90 kHz away
from the setpoint after retries are also saved as `nan`. Each row is flushed immediately;
an interrupted run retains completed rows. A completed run includes a final
comment with row count, valid peak count and elapsed time. An existing output
file is replaced. Remote sequences and playback logs remain in the printed
`/home/root/sweep1.*` directory for inspection.

At completion CH1 remains at **200 MHz and amplitude code 102**. The analyzer
returns to continuous scanning at the final center frequency. On interruption
or error, CH1 holds its last programmed setting.

The generator also exposes the per-point operation independently:

```sh
./spi_ad9959 --set-ch1 point.seq 200e6 102
```

`--set-ch1 output.seq CH1_Hz amplitude_code` requires prior standard DDS
initialization. It updates CH1 only, without resetting the DDS/PLL; frequency
and amplitude latch together. This mode accepts DC through 200 MHz and
integer amplitude codes 0..1023. The ordinary four-tone initialization still
requires strictly positive frequencies.
